u brian may bartis  chagger  valerie bradford and of course the host  mp tim tim lewis prime minister our area  could not be uh better represented by a  wonderful team and over the past couple  of years the support that municipalities  like kitchener and the region of  waterloo have had uh with covet 19 the  safe restart funding and so on has been  extremely important and i really just  want to say the team here have been rock  stars and thank you to to you and all of  them  but can just maybe say a few words about  the relationship that cities like  kitchener and the region of waterloo  have had with the government of canada  over the past number of years uh  together with the federation of canadian  municipalities we've been able to  achieve a great deal in fact it was in  your first term as prime minister that  we were able to deli that the government  was able to deliver on the national  housing strategy something that cities  like kitchener were calling for for many  years  and and in addition to that we were also  calling for a program like the rapid  housing initiative and sure enough in  the second term the shared goal of the  rapid housing initiative focused around  eliminating homelessness over a number  of years uh was a program that your  government listened to cities about and  in fact  created the program and created  thousands of units across canada  this initiative is in fact changing  lives in the city of kitchener and  throughout waterloo region  just a couple of examples  for example indigenous families and  individuals moving into 30 units  that are being built by the kw urban  native wigwam project in cambridge and  we'll see women moving into over 40  units at the yw partnership in the city  of kitchener that's happening all of  this is happening through the rapid  housing initiative  budget 2022 once again listen to the  voices of cities and communities and  we're very appreciative as cities of the  many investments that the federal  government has made  with respect to  housing and as cities and communities  we're ready to roll up our sleeves in  order to get that money flowing and  making a difference in the lives of  of peoples in our community so when the  next phase of rapid housing initiative  funding starts flowing we'll be ready as  we were in the first two phases to get  those units built and we're certainly  ready to work together with the federal  government and the four billion dollars  in the federal housing accelerator fund  with the goal of building a hundred  thousand new units in the next five  years  prime minister you were absolutely right  when you said canada and canadians are  best served when the orders of  government work together on behalf of  canadians i think we've demonstrated  that over the last number of years and i  can tell you as one city in one region  i'm ready with my colleagues to roll up  our sleeves in the cities townships in  the region here to uh to work together  with you and make make a difference for  our future and i'll turn it back to you  [Applause]  thank you we now have time for questions  from the media  media  we have one question one follow-up for  each reporter please identify your  outlet and your name thank you we can  start with the first question  hi this is hala with cbc  i'm just wondering well the heavy  artillery you announced yesterday come  from the canadian army inventory will  you provide ammunition and will that be  guided ammunition as well and when will  you commit to replace these and other  weapons taken from canadian inventories  in english and french please  from the very beginning of russia's  illegal invasion of ukraine and indeed  uh in in the weeks before  we've been there for ukraine we've been  sending  lethal and non-lethal military  assistance we've been there for  humanitarian support significant  financial support for the people in the  government of ukraine as well as  they deal with this unconscionable  illegal invasion by uh by russia  we have every step of the way responded  to  the requests and the specific asks of  the ukrainian government of president  zielinski who i speak with whom i speak  regularly and their most recent ask was  exactly for that for heavy artillery  for  reasons of operational security i can't  go into the details uh at this point on  uh how and what we're getting to them  exactly but i can assure you we will  have more to say in the coming days as  we deliver what they actually need to be  able to continue not just to defend  their own homeland against this illegal  russian invasion but to defend the very  values and principles that we hold dear  in canada and around the world as free  and open democracies  [Music]  [Music]  and just a quick follow-up  do you have any idea when these weapons  or others taken from canadian  inventories will be replaced  the ability of canada to contribute  fully to nato is one that is extremely  important for us  and every step of the way as we support  ukraine including with military  equipment and munitions  we are also making sure that canada  continues to have the capacity to  operate not just to defend canada but to  participate fully in nato operations as  well  okay next question  my prime minister i'm stephanie with ctv  kitchener  um so russia has claimed that there are  canadians fighting in mariupol right now  is the canadian government aware of any  canadians fighting for ukraine there and  will there be any assistance given if  they are captured  we know uh that canadians feel very  strongly about supporting people in  ukraine uh across the country people  have been opening up their homes their  communities uh to  support ukrainians either fleeing war or  supporting them through donations and  through delivery of of  of equipment and supports  we also know that a number of canadians  have chosen to go and fight  alongside ukrainians either because of  their own ukrainian heritage or because  they want to help stand up for democracy  against this illegal russian uh invasion  uh there are no active uh you know  forces being deployed uh by canada in  ukraine obviously but there are  certainly individual citizens who've  chosen uh to contribute  we will always be there to try and help  canadians but what we said from the very  beginning was canadians  should be leaving ukraine uh because  we're going to be very limited in our  capacity to help them uh if things go  wrong  follow-up yeah it's uh similar to the  question that my friend just asked you  but  a member of the ukrainian parliament  tweeted this morning saying they're now  starting to receive delivery of heavy  artillery from western countries when  should canadians expect that the heavy  artillery canada ascending to a rise in  ukraine i think people understand that  delivery of military equipment  in the  context of an illegal war by russia is  something that we have to be a little  bit  careful about what we broadcast and  publicize and what we're doing uh but in  the coming days i certainly hope to be  able to share more about what is being  sent what was sent  but i can reassure canadians that we are  directly responding to the top requests  that the ukrainians have given for  assistance  in this current phase of the war which  is uh a redoubled efforts by uh russia  uh to take uh take donbass and therefore  there is very specific equipment that  ukrainians may need and canadians can be  comfortable that their government is  there to support ukrainians with what  they need next question  good morning prime minister jeff out  from the waterloo region record i want  to return to housing for a second here  my son 25 years old still lives at home  with us college grad good paying job  he laughs off the idea that he'll ever  own a house a detached house like the  one he lives in now that we were able to  afford 25 years ago he just thinks it's  a joke the housing prices are ridiculous  there's no way he's ever going to be  able to afford the kind of mortgage it  would take crippling for life  so i want to ask your thoughts on that  those uh you know people starting out in  their 20s and early 30s uh what do you  say to them do you say give up on  detached ownership and go for something  smaller and it's a dream that's dead  you're never going to get there let me  ask how do you feel about it uh  obviously i've heard from uh canadians  like your son uh across the country who  are really worried about the housing  market who wonder whether or not they'll  ever be able to afford a home one of the  challenges we're facing in canada is our  population with immigration and other  things has been growing over the past  years and housing construction hasn't  kept up  which  is a real problem and that's why one of  the initiatives we're working on four  billion dollars towards municipalities  in order to double the construction of  new housing over the coming years  this is something that is going to help  uh whether it's enough or directly going  to be helping your son we'll have to see  because there's not just one program  that's going to help anyone there's  going to be a range of programs from  programs of renting to own that we've  launched as well in this budget from  programs that are helping the first time  home buyers with increased incentives  and reduction in their more mortgage  costs  initiatives to build greater density  particularly around public transit that  are going to allow people to get into  the housing market with their first  starter condo starter home these are the  kinds of things that get people's toes  in the door  we know that young people need to be  able to  build the stable base with which they  want to start a family give their future  kids the kinds of opportunities that  their parents worked hard to give them  and that's what this budget and this  focus of the government is on right now  but it's not going to be easy  it's going to take a number of years and  anyone who's promising that they have a  quick fix for it  is not being straight with canadians  it's going to take all of us working  together to curb foreign speculation to  make sure that market is fairer to  create more housing units and offer and  to support families being able to save  up to do that and that's very much what  we're focused on thank you prime  minister one follow-up question  different topic we do not currently have  a member of parliament a member of  parliament in this region in cabinet  with the departure of bartis jagger from  cabinet recently is uh  why is that and will your government  rectify that important area and we've  had cabinet membership for many years we  continue to have an amazing team of  strong mps and ministers from across the  country who are focused on this region  on the growing  challenges and opportunities in southern  ontario and i can assure you  that all of us do a great job making  sure we're speaking to and for all  canadians but as always we're looking at  to grow the teams in coming elections  and we're going to continue to stay  connected with canadians and build the  kind of team that is responsive to what  canadians need next question  good morning prime minister liam casey  with the canadian press um major group  of uh major group helping veterans um  transition network to get interpreters  out of afghanistan they're winding down  operations  you know because they say there's  bureaucratic cubes that are blocking  efforts to get paperwork and causing  this bottleneck  what do you have to say to the  interpreters who risked their lives to  help canada and now feel  abandoned by your government the  situation in afghanistan is  heartbreaking  the  taking over that country by the taliban  has made it unbelievably difficult  not just for canadians who served and  sacrificed to build a better future for  afghans but also to people who want to  welcome people who've supported them  to canada we have  accepted and been able to uh draw to  canada well over 10 000  people fleeing afghanistan we are on our  way to 40 000. the challenges we are  facing however are very much linked to  the difficulty of actually processing  people from within afghanistan because  as might be imagined the taliban are not  exactly being helpful in terms of  bringing people out of afghanistan to  canada we continue to work with partners  in the region and with allies around the  world because we remain committed to  welcoming over 40 000 afghan  refugees translators people who've  worked with canadians  human rights defenders  while at the same time we continue to be  committed to humanitarian support within  afghanistan to make sure that  opportunities continue to be there for  people who remain  thank you  another topic there have been several  attacks of late in and around mosques  the imam of  dar al tahit  in mississauga where there was a man  with an axe and unleashed bear spray  they say the federal government's  dragging their feet on the federal  security infrastructure program that  would help them  you know beef up security they're  wondering when that money  will be coming  so that they can get things in security  guards and  surveillance equipment to help their  members  over  over the past number of years we have  continued to invest in the community  infrastructure program for  mosques and synagogues and and  vulnerable community centers and we will  continue to be there with supports  but it's a really big shame that we have  to  canada shouldn't be a place where we  have to worry about  fences and armed guards and video  cameras and security systems  in places of worship  canada is one of those countries that  does diversity and inclusivity better  than just about any other country in the  world  but over the past years we've seen a  rise  in hate crimes  a rise in intolerance  and racist acts  part of it is the pandemic sure  and the and the stress and the anxiety  that comes from that  but a part of it is a trend  towards greater polarization in our  politics greater intolerance  in our communities  that the vast vast majority of canadians  stand against  islamophobia  anti-semitism anti-black racism  anti-lgbt  all the all the discriminations  that we see on the rise anti-asian  racism through through the pandemic  are absolutely unacceptable  and as a government and quite frankly as  canadians  we will continue to stand together with  vulnerable communities  and demonstrate that there is no place  for hatred and hateful acts  in canada whether  in real life or online and we're going  to continue  to move forward to make sure that all  canadians are safe and i look forward to  sitting down with the muslim community  here in the kitchener-waterloo region  later this afternoon to talk about the  amazing things they're doing  to contribute to the community while  they celebrate the holy month of ramadan  last question please  thank you hello mr prime minister uh my  question has to do again with housing i  was looking at the rapid housing  investments we have 1.5 billion dollars  over two years and a quarter of that  going to  women-focused programming but there are  still concerns regarding a lack of  targeted investment specifically for  indigenous people that we know are  disproportionately impacted by housing  and security what's your government  doing to ensure that marginalized  communities and indigenous individuals  aren't overlooked by these new housing  investments  from the very beginning of  canada's first ever national housing  strategy that we put forward in in 2017  which at that point was 40 billion  dollars is now up to about 72 billion  dollars investments in housing there  were specific targets and carveouts for  indigenous canadians uh for for women  for vulnerable communities for canadians  with disabilities these are the kinds of  things that we've built into the  programs we have and even as we move  forward on the rapid housing initiative  as we move forward on partnerships  with with municipalities there are  specific supports for the most  vulnerable  there are often women and young families  fleeing domestic violence  marginalized canadians who don't have  the same access to credit i think of  black canadians and others  where systemic racism continues to be a  challenge so we are explicit about  making sure that there is a focus on  those communities and we will continue  to it is extremely important that we be  there uh to ensure that everyone uh can  benefit from this and leaning in on  canadians facing greater barriers is uh  core to it i can also say that we have  specific urban sorry urban indigenous  and indigenous housing programs uh to  the tunes of billions of dollars that  we've put forward in this budget as a  government we will continue to  specifically work with uh indigenous  communities to build a path forward on  reconciliation that brings everyone  along  my follow-up is just regarding the uh  foreign buyer ban the two-year ban uh  there has already been criticism levy  that there may still be loopholes  regarding um ways to work around the  restrictions that are even put in place  by this budget um what's your government  doing to make sure that you're able to  address those loopholes quickly as  opposed to just you know where there's  opportunity people are going to try and  find ways to exploit it  we are very aware as we move forward  with strong measures to protect  canadians there are going to be people  trying to get around them whether it was  our measures to bring in better tax  fairness by lowering taxes for the  middle class and raising them on the  wealthiest one percent uh whether it's  measures to support people through the  pandemic unfortunately there are always  going to be people trying to find ways  around it that's why in the design work  on the  on the foreign buyers ban uh that we  will be bringing in we're going to make  sure that it applies as broadly and as  as completely as possible because uh we  don't we need to actually address this  problem and we don't need people getting  around it thank you that concludes  today's press conference
 

